{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-65252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"} {"question" "q-65252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ is defined only when $xy>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}(x,y)=1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ is defined only when $xy>0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"} {"question" "q-65252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "578181504269c8dd2d8c21021e6855bf596ae57fac021f07d6446ef20d8cb22a"} {"question" "q-65252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"} {"question" "q-65252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy<0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy\\ge 0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"} {"question" "q-65252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"} {"question" "q-65255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=xy{e}^{x/y}?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(0,1)$is defined\n- ${f}_{y}(1,0)$ is defined\n- Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"} {"question" "q-65255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =y{e}^{x/y}+xy{e}^{x/y}(\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =y{e}^{x/y}+x{e}^{x/y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{x/y}+xy{e}^{x/y}(-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{x/y}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}{e}^{x/y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(0,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,1)=0\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{0/1}=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1)=1\\cdot {e}^{0/1}+0\\cdot {e}^{0/1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into ${f}_{y}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{y}(1,0)=1\\cdot {e}^{1/0}-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{0}\\cdot {e}^{1/0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,1)=1\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}=e,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}+1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}=2e \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,1) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{1}\\cdot {e}^{1/1}=e-e=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"} {"question" "q-65255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "578181504269c8dd2d8c21021e6855bf596ae57fac021f07d6446ef20d8cb22a"} {"question" "q-65255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"} {"question" "q-65255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"} {"question" "q-65255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"} {"question" "q-72027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{1+y-{x}^{2}}}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{xz}{\\sqrt{(1+y-{x}^{2})^{3}}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{(1+y-{x}^{2})^{3}}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+y-{x}^{2}}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"} {"question" "q-72027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{1+y-{x}^{2}}}$ is defined only when $1+y-{x}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}=\\frac{xz}{\\sqrt{(1+y-{x}^{2})^{3}}}$ is defined only when $1+y-{x}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ and ${f}_{z}$ are the same\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={D}_{{f}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"} {"question" "q-72027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "578181504269c8dd2d8c21021e6855bf596ae57fac021f07d6446ef20d8cb22a"} {"question" "q-72027" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"} {"question" "q-72027" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}>y+1}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\ge y+1}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\le y+1}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\ne y+1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"} {"question" "q-72027" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"} {"question" "q-132649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{y}\\,{e}^{x/y}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{{e}^{x/y}}{\\sqrt{y}},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{(y-2x)\\,{e}^{x/y}}{2\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $g$ exist." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"} {"question" "q-132649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{y}\\,{e}^{x/y}$ is defined only when $y>0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}={(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{x/y}}{\\sqrt{y}}$ is defined only when $y>0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${g}_{y}(x,y)$ is the same: \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ge 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"} {"question" "q-132649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "578181504269c8dd2d8c21021e6855bf596ae57fac021f07d6446ef20d8cb22a"} {"question" "q-132649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"} {"question" "q-132649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y\\ge 0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0,\\,y>0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ge 0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"} {"question" "q-132649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"} {"question" "q-132680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}?$\n\n- Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(4,2)$\n- ${f}_{x}(0,2)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{y}(0,2)$ is defined" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"} {"question" "q-132680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(4,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(4,2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{4}}{{2}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(4,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(4,2) & =\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})\\sqrt{4}}=\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ {f}_{y}(4,2) & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{4}}{{2}^{3}}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,2)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,2)=\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})\\sqrt{0}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(0,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{0}}{{2}^{2}}=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(0,2)=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{0}}{{2}^{3}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"} {"question" "q-132680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "578181504269c8dd2d8c21021e6855bf596ae57fac021f07d6446ef20d8cb22a"} {"question" "q-132680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"} {"question" "q-132680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"} {"question" "q-132680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068517/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fdf61b95cb0e52ae880fc5e01e5eacc809b69001eb56a3e48a47be7c0ed79b"}] "usage" {"q-65252" []} "basis" 2302 "retractions" [[17592186125109 :question/problem "Given that $f(x, y) = x\\ln (xy)$ and\n\n$\\frac{∂f}{∂x} = 1 + \\ln (xy), \\frac{∂f}{∂y} = \\frac{x}{y}$,\nfind the set of values $(x, y)$ where all partial derivatives of $f$ exist."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186123677 17592186123678 17592186123679 17592186123680 17592186123681] "immutable_fields" [[17592186123676 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123676 :answer-field/id] [17592186123676 :answer-field/key] [17592186123676 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
